  <h1>Negotiating Job Offers</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Evaluate before responding</h2><p>Always ask for time to consider (&ldquo;This is exciting — can I have a few days to review the details?&rdquo;). 24–72 hours is standard. Use the time to research, compare to your market rate, and decide what you&rsquo;ll ask for. Never accept verbally on the spot; written acceptance comes later.</p>
  <h2>Structure your counter</h2><p>Make your counter in one shot — don&rsquo;t negotiate piecemeal. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m very excited about this opportunity. Based on my research and experience, I&rsquo;d like to ask for $X in base, a $Y signing bonus, and Z additional vacation days. If you can meet me there, I&rsquo;m ready to sign.&rdquo; Giving them a clear path to yes speeds the process.</p>
  <h2>Multiple offers</h2><p>If you have multiple offers, you can disclose this without revealing the specific amounts. &ldquo;I have another offer I&rsquo;m evaluating — I&rsquo;d love to work here if we can get close to market.&rdquo; Never fabricate competing offers; this can be verified and damages trust.</p>
  <h2>When to stop negotiating</h2><p>If the employer has countered twice and you&rsquo;re still below your target, decide: take it, or decline gracefully. Negotiating more than two rounds rarely moves the needle and can sour the relationship. Know your walk-away number before starting.</p>
  <h2>Declining professionally</h2><p>If you decline, do so promptly and with a brief, honest reason (&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve accepted another offer&rdquo; or &ldquo;the total compensation didn&rsquo;t align with my expectations&rdquo;). Industry is small; you may encounter this recruiter again. Bridges matter.</p>
